# The vibrator this device actually has.
#
# 🔴 Found 2026-08-19, once the vibrator HAL started running for the first time
# (its -stock rename had no exec type until today, so it never ran to be denied).
#
# There are TWO vibrator nodes here and MediaTek labels only the simple one:
#   sepolicy_vndr/basic/non_plat/genfs_contexts:268
#     /devices/platform/soc/soc:regulator_vibrator/leds/vibrator -> sysfs_vibrator
# Stock's HAL drives the other one -- an I2C AWINIC-class LRA at 0-0058, with
# f0/cali/lra_resistance/osc_cali/ram_update/rtp/gain, i.e. real haptics rather
# than a on/off motor -- and nothing labelled it, so it landed as plain sysfs:
#   avc: denied { read write } for comm="android.hardwar" name="activate"
#     scontext=u:r:hal_vibrator_default:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:sysfs:s0
#
# Only the LABEL is missing. MediaTek already grants the permission:
#   sepolicy_vndr/basic/non_plat/hal_vibrator_default.te
#     allow hal_vibrator_default sysfs_vibrator:file rw_file_perms;
# Confirmed live with chcon on the running device before this was written: the
# denials stopped and did not come back.
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/11e00000.i2c/i2c-0/0-0058/leds/vibrator_single u:object_r:sysfs_vibrator:s0

# /proc/sys/vm/dirty_writeback_centisecs -- the third of three sibling knobs,
# and the one AOSP forgot to label:
#   system/sepolicy/private/genfs_contexts:82  /sys/vm/dirty_background_ratio  -> proc_dirty
#   system/sepolicy/private/genfs_contexts:83  /sys/vm/dirty_expire_centisecs  -> proc_dirty
#   (no entry at all for dirty_writeback_centisecs)
#
# Unlabelled it resolves to plain `proc`, which vendor_init may open, read,
# setattr and map but NOT write (system/sepolicy/public/vendor_init.te:143).
# So stock's own tuning line, which this tree extracts verbatim --
#   rootdir/etc/init/hw/init.mt6789.rc:821  write /proc/sys/vm/dirty_writeback_centisecs 300
#   (stock's init.mt6789.rc:800, byte for byte)
# -- fails silently. Measured on build 71: one avc denial at boot, and
#   /proc/sys/vm/dirty_writeback_centisecs = 500
# which is the kernel default, not the 300 stock asks for. init reports no error
# for a failed `write`, so nothing surfaces it.
#
# Labelling it proc_dirty is what its two siblings already carry, and no
# neverallow names proc_dirty or restricts writing proc_type. Labelling a
# /proc/sys/vm node from vendor policy follows MediaTek's own precedent:
#   sepolicy_vndr/basic/non_plat/genfs_contexts:125
#     genfscon proc /sys/vm/swappiness u:object_r:proc_swappiness:s0
genfscon proc /sys/vm/dirty_writeback_centisecs u:object_r:proc_dirty:s0
